#Start-Script 
./FADE IN:
.@ACT ONE
.\EXT. HOLLOWAY STATION - PLATFORM - NIGHT
>%Setup: Nora misses the 11:40 and meets the conductor
.%who says there's one more train.
>.Rain hammers a tin roof. A single sodium lamp buzzes over an
>.empty platform. The departure board flickers: every train
..CANCELLED.
>.NORA PIKE (30s, soaked, a briefcase held like a shield)
>.sprints up the stairs just in time to watch red tail lights
..vanish into the dark.
._NORA
.:No. No, no, no.
..She kicks a bench. It kicks back. She hops in pain.
>.Behind her, a lantern swings into view. OLD BEN (70s,
>.conductor's cap older than he is) watches her with mild
..amusement.
._OLD BEN
.:That was the eleven-forty.
._NORA
.((through gritted teeth)
.:I'd gathered.
._OLD BEN
>:There's one more. Doesn't stop here
.:often. Only when someone needs it.
._NORA
.:Is that supposed to be comforting?
..A distant WHISTLE. Low and mournful. Ben smiles.
./CUT TO:
.\EXT. RAILWAY LINE - CONTINUOUS
>%An establishing shot of the impossible train. No
.%summary needed beyond the image.
.=WIDE ON THE TRACKS
>.Out of the fog comes a steam locomotive that has no business
>.existing in this century. Brass gleams. Every window glows
..amber.
.\INT. LAST TRAIN - CORRIDOR - NIGHT
>.Nora steps aboard, dripping on a carpet that looks like it
..has never been stepped on. The doors HISS shut behind her.
>.The corridor is lined with compartments. Each door has a
>.brass number plate. None of the numbers are in order: 7, 2,
..19, 4.
._NORA
.:Hello? Ticket office? Anyone?
..A compartment door slides open on its own. Number 13.
.\INT. LAST TRAIN - COMPARTMENT 13 - NIGHT
>%Nora meets Iris, who knows her name. First hint the
.%passengers are people Nora has lost touch with.
>.A cosy compartment. Velvet seats. A tea service, still
>.steaming. Across from the empty seat sits IRIS VANCE (60s,
..cardigan, knitting something enormous).
._IRIS
>:Sit down, Nora, you're making the
.:tea nervous.
._NORA
.:How do you know my name?
._IRIS
.((not looking up)
>:You used to sit on my front step
>:and read my newspapers before I'd
>:had a chance to. Thirty years and
.:you still don't knock.
..Nora stares. Slowly sits.
._NORA
>:Mrs. Vance? You moved away when I
.:was nine.
._IRIS
>:I moved on. Different thing
.:entirely.
./DISSOLVE TO:
.@ACT TWO
.\INT. LAST TRAIN - DINING CAR - NIGHT
>%Midpoint: the dining car is full of faces from Nora's
>%past. She realises every passenger is someone she owes
.%an apology.
>.Every table is occupied. Nora freezes in the doorway. She
>.knows these people. A school friend. A former boss. Her
..brother DANNY (30s), who raises a glass without smiling.
._DANNY
.:Look who finally caught a train.
._NORA
.:Danny. I meant to call.
._DANNY
.:For six years?
>.Silence. A waiter glides past carrying a single cupcake with
..a single candle.
._DANNY (CONT'D)
>:It was my birthday last week. In
.:case you're keeping track.
.\INT. LAST TRAIN - CORRIDOR - LATER
>.Nora walks fast. The compartment numbers rearrange
..themselves as she passes: 13 becomes 31 becomes 3.
._NORA
.((to herself)
>:It's a dream. A stress dream. You
.:fell asleep on the platform.
>.Old Ben appears beside her, punching tickets that aren't
..there.
._OLD BEN
.:They all say that. Never helps.
.\INT. LAST TRAIN - LUGGAGE VAN - NIGHT
>%Nora finds her own briefcase stacked a hundred times
>%over: every unanswered letter and unsent message she's
.%ever written.
>.Mountains of luggage. Hundreds of identical briefcases. All
..hers.
>.She opens one. Letters spill out, all addressed in her
..handwriting, none of them sent.
.=INSERT - A LETTER
>."Dear Danny, I'm sorry I missed it. Work was..." The rest is
..crossed out.
._NORA
.:I never sent any of these.
._OLD BEN (O.S.)
.:That's why they're so heavy.
.\EXT. LAST TRAIN - ROOF - NIGHT - CONTINUOUS
>.Wind roars. Nora climbs onto the roof, letters stuffed in
>.every pocket. The train races across a viaduct over a sea of
..fog.
>.Below, in the fog, the lights of a hundred tiny stations
..wink in and out. Every place she ever said she'd visit.
._NORA
.((shouting into the wind)
.:Where does this train go?
._OLD BEN (O.S.)
>:Wherever you're willing to go back
.:to!
./SMASH CUT TO:
.@ACT THREE
.\INT. LAST TRAIN - DINING CAR - NIGHT
>%Climax: Nora delivers the letters by hand and finally
.%apologises to Danny.
>.Nora walks table to table, handing out the letters. Each
>.passenger reads theirs. Some laugh. One cries. Iris nods,
..satisfied, and keeps knitting.
..Finally, Danny. She puts the last letter in front of him.
._DANNY
.:What's this?
._NORA
>:Six years of me being an idiot.
.:Abridged.
..He reads. His jaw tightens. Then he laughs, despite himself.
._DANNY
.:You spelled "birthday" wrong.
._NORA
.:I was upset.
..He stands. They hug, awkward and long overdue.
.\INT. LAST TRAIN - VESTIBULE - NIGHT
..Old Ben waits by the door with his lantern. The train slows.
._OLD BEN
.:Your stop.
._NORA
.:Will I see any of them again?
._OLD BEN
.:That's rather up to you, isn't it?
.\EXT. HOLLOWAY STATION - PLATFORM - DAWN
>%Resolution: back on the platform at dawn, Nora makes a
.%phone call instead of waiting for a train.
>.Sunlight. Birds. The departure board reads ON TIME for every
..train.
>.Nora sits on the bench she kicked. Her briefcase is lighter.
..She takes out her phone and dials.
._NORA
.((into phone)
>:Danny? It's me. Yeah, I know what
>:time it is. Listen, are you free
.:for lunch? I'll come to you.
>.Behind her, faintly, a train WHISTLE. She doesn't turn
..around.
./FADE OUT.
